{"type":"WebSearch","query":"PPIC statewide survey October 2026 Proposition 43 yes no","content":"**In the PPIC Statewide Survey (October 2026, conducted Oct. 16–22 among 1,086 likely voters), 42% said they would vote yes on Proposition 43 and 55% said they would vote no (3% undecided).**[[1]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

Proposition 43 is a legislative constitutional amendment titled “Limits Voters’ Ability to Raise Revenues for Local Government Services.” It would raise the voter-approval threshold for local special taxes (including citizen-initiated ones) from a simple majority to two-thirds, starting in 2027. Support was below 50% across regions and varied by party (28% Democrats, 68% Republicans, 41% independents) and age. This was similar to the September PPIC survey (43% yes, 51% no).[[1]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

The survey’s margin of error for likely voters is ±3.8%. Full details, crosstabs, and methodology are available on the PPIC website.","citations":["https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"site:calmatters.org Proposition 43 2026 polling likely to pass or fail","content":"**Proposition 43 (2026) would raise the voter-approval threshold for citizen-initiated local special taxes (those dedicated to a specific purpose, like sales or property taxes for services) from a simple majority to two-thirds, starting in 2027.** It does not affect taxes proposed by local governments (already requiring two-thirds) or general taxes. CalMatters describes it as a compromise measure placed on the ballot by the Legislature after a deal with anti-tax groups like the Howard Jarvis Taxpayers Association.[[1]](https://calmatters.org/politics/2026/10/california-prop-43-election-2026/)

CalMatters’ reporting on polling, based on a September 2026 Public Policy Institute of California (PPIC) survey of likely voters, showed **51% would vote no versus 43% yes**. Voters appeared to be leaning against it. Supporters (including business groups) hoped it would appeal to concerns about California’s high cost of living and affordability, while opponents (including unions and local officials) argued it would hinder funding for essential services like fire response.[[1]](https://calmatters.org/politics/2026/10/california-prop-43-election-2026/)

CalMatters’ later articles (through late October 2026, including a last-minute voter guide) did not cite updated polls specifically for Prop. 43, though they noted public polling was tight on some tax measures overall. Based on the available CalMatters-cited data, it was polling below the 50%+ majority needed to pass and thus **leaning toward failure**. Independent later polls (not from CalMatters) showed even stronger opposition. The measure requires a simple majority of votes cast on it.","citations":["https://calmatters.org/politics/2026/10/california-prop-43-election-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}
